                                "sense_id": "9c6925db-74b3-38e5-af0c-dc4c9253dbe5",
                                "text": "1977 O.W. Richards & R.G. Davies Imms' general textbook of entomology pub. Methuen\nPeripneustic... spiracles in a row along each side of the body. In typical examples the prothoracic and abdominal spiracles are open, that of the metathorax being closed. This condition is found in the terrestrial larvae of the orders Neuroptera, Mecoptera, Lepidoptera, of many Hymenoptera Symphyta, and of many Coleoptera..."
